// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/proc/scheduler.vuma
// VWK Process/Thread Subsystem — CFS-like scheduler (runqueue + vruntime)
// (PMT-pure).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the K4b layer of the VWK kernel: the runqueue (a singly
// linked list of Task indices into the ProcessTable) and the CFS-like
// scheduler that picks the task with the lowest vruntime. K4a (task.vuma)
// built the Task control block + ProcessTable + task_alloc/task_free;
// K4b consumes ProcessTable (re-declared here) to track which tasks are
// runnable and to pick the next one to run.
//
// ── CFS-like scheduling ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The Completely Fair Scheduler (Linux's default since 2.6.23) gives every
// runnable task a fair share of CPU time by tracking each task's "virtual
// runtime" (vruntime) — a nanosecond-precision counter of CPU time the
// task has consumed, weighted by its priority. The scheduler picks the
// task with the LOWEST vruntime (the one that has had the least CPU time
// recently). K4b implements a simplified CFS:
//   * The runqueue is a singly linked list of Task indices (0..255),
//     threaded through Task.next (the same `next` field K4a's ProcessTable
//     exposes via pt_get_next / pt_set_next). The list head/tail live in
//     the Runqueue struct. Tail-insertion (sched_enqueue) gives FIFO
//     ordering for tasks with equal vruntimes — same as Linux's rbtree
//     leftmost-with-tie-break-on-insertion-order behavior.
//   * sched_dequeue walks the entire runqueue, finds the task with min
//     vruntime, removes it from the list, and returns its index. (Linux
//     uses an rbtree for O(log n) min-vruntime lookup; K4b uses a linear
//     scan for O(n) — simpler and adequate for the 256-slot ProcessTable.)
//   * schedule(rq, tbl) is just sched_dequeue — the CFS "pick next" step.
//   * sched_tick(tbl, task_idx, delta) bumps the current task's vruntime
//     by delta (called by the timer IRQ handler on every tick).
//   * sched_yield(rq, tbl, current) re-enqueues the current task and
//     picks the next one (cooperative yield).
//
// ── Task states used by the scheduler ───────────────────────────────────────
// Same values as K4a's task.vuma:
//     RUNNING = 1  — currently running on a CPU (set by sched_dequeue)
//     READY   = 2  — runnable, waiting for a CPU (set by sched_enqueue)
// state == 0 means the slot is FREE (unused — not on any runqueue).
// sched_enqueue writes state = 2 (READY); sched_dequeue writes state = 1
// (RUNNING) on the picked task before returning it. K4e (exit/wait) will
// write state = 4 (ZOMBIE) on sys_exit and remove the task from the
// runqueue via sched_dequeue-style list removal.
//
// ── Runqueue layout ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   head:  u32 — index of the first task in the runqueue, or 256 = empty
//   tail:  u32 — index of the last task in the runqueue, or 256 = empty
//   count: u32 — number of tasks currently in the runqueue (0..256)
// head/tail are u32 (not u64) to match ProcessTable's index type —
// pt_set_next takes idx: u32, and rq.head / rq.tail are passed directly
// to pt_set_next as the prev-of-new-tail argument. 256 is the "empty"
// sentinel: it's outside the valid 0..255 Task-index range, so it can't
// collide with a real Task slot. Same sentinel value K4a's task_alloc
// returns when the table is full (so callers can use one `idx == 256`
// check for "no task" regardless of the call site).
//
// The runqueue's `next` linkage lives in ProcessTable.nexts (not in
// Runqueue itself) — K4a's Task layout already has a `next` field for
// exactly this purpose (see K4a's task.vuma header comment on the `next`
// field: "next task in a linked list (arena offset or 0; K4b uses this
// for the run queue)"). The sentinel value used in Task.next is also 256
// (NOT 0 — K4a's comment says "0 = end of list" but K4b standardizes on
// 256 for consistency with the rest of the scheduler's empty-sentinel
// convention; both work because Task 0 is a real task, but 256 is never
// a real task).
//
// ── ProcessTable re-declaration ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// VUMA has no `import` (Open Work §7), so this file re-declares the
// ProcessTable layout byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma. The byte-
// identical-redeclaration invariant (K2c "extern signatures MUST stay
// byte-identical" — extended to layouts in K3d) extends to ProcessTable:
// this file's pt_get_vruntime / pt_set_vruntime / pt_get_next /
// pt_set_next MUST see the same field offsets, field names, and field
// types as K4a's task.vuma. ProcessTable has 10 fields (9 parallel flat
// [u8; N] arrays + a count u32) totaling 14340 bytes — same as K4a.
//
// This file also re-declares the 6 ProcessTable helpers it needs:
//   pt_get_state / pt_set_state   (u8 direct-index — no pack/unpack)
//   pt_get_vruntime / pt_set_vruntime (u64 8-byte LE pack/unpack)
//   pt_get_next / pt_set_next     (u64 8-byte LE pack/unpack)
// These 6 helpers are byte-identical to K4a's task.vuma (same signature,
// same body — 8-iteration while loop for u64, direct array index for u8).
// The other 12 helpers (pid/ppid get/set, prio get/set, mm_root/fs_root/
// fds get/set) are NOT re-declared here because K4b's scheduler doesn't
// use them — K4d (fork/exec) will re-declare those when it needs them.
//
// ── Why init-style? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The K4b contract's main() allocates `rq = state_new(Runqueue)` and
// `tbl = state_new(ProcessTable)` and passes them by reference to
// sched_init / sched_enqueue / sched_dequeue / sched_tick / sched_yield.
// This is the canonical PMT pattern (K2a's pmm_init(pool, pmm, ...),
// K3d's trap_frame_init(tf), K4a's pt_init(tbl) — see K2a's pmm.vuma
// header "Why init-style?" for the codegen rationale: the current
// codegen does NOT propagate State-typedness through function return
// values, so `fn sched_new() -> State<Runqueue>` would produce broken
// field-access code in callers). All scheduler.vuma functions take
// `rq: State<Runqueue>` and/or `tbl: State<ProcessTable>` by regular
// VUMA-fn borrow (NOT extern #[borrow] — VUMA fn calls don't need the
// attribute; only extern "C" calls do, and none of these functions call
// any extern).
//
// State<T> params: per the K4b contract's "Keep State<T> params to 1-2
// per function" rule, no function takes more than 2 State<T> params:
//   sched_init(rq)                     — 1 param
//   sched_enqueue(rq, tbl, task_idx)   — 2 params + u32
//   sched_dequeue(rq, tbl)             — 2 params
//   schedule(rq, tbl)                  — 2 params (wraps sched_dequeue)
//   sched_tick(tbl, task_idx, delta)   — 1 param + u32 + u64
//   sched_yield(rq, tbl, current)      — 2 params + u32
// Same discipline as K2a's pmm_alloc(pool, pmm, order) (2 State params +
// 1 small arg), K3d's trap_frame_save(tf) (1 State param).
//
// ── Linked-list traversal pattern ───────────────────────────────────────────
// sched_dequeue walks the runqueue linked list to find the min-vruntime
// task, tracking both `prev` (the node before `cur`) and `prev_of_min`
// (the node before `min_idx`). This is byte-identical in structure to
// K2a's pmm_free buddy-search loop (pmm.vuma lines ~475-490: `let prev =
// 256; let cur = ...; let found = 256; while cur != 256 { if match
// { found = cur; } prev = cur; cur = next; }`), with the addition of
// tracking `prev_of_min` so the removal step can relink the predecessor.
// The `256` sentinel for "no previous" / "not found" matches pmm.vuma's
// convention.
//
// ── u64 / u32 mixed arithmetic ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// `pt_get_next` returns u64 (because Task.next is u64), so the list-
// traversal cursor `cur` is u64. The `while cur != 256` loop compares
// u64 to the literal 256 (inferred as u64 in this context — same as
// pmm.vuma's `while cur2 != 256`). When `cur` is passed to pt_get_vruntime
// / pt_get_next (which take idx: u32), the contract uses an explicit
// `cur as u32` cast. Similarly, `min_idx = cur as u32` and `prev = cur
// as u32` cast the u64 cursor back to u32 for storage. This u64-cursor /
// u32-index pattern is byte-identical to pmm.vuma's `pool_get_base(pool,
// cur2 as u32)` / `pool_get_next(pool, cur2 as u32)` calls.
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. Both
// Runqueue and ProcessTable are ___pmt_buffer-backed State<T>'s
// allocated by the CALLER via `state_new(...)` and passed by reference
// to sched_init / sched_enqueue / sched_dequeue / sched_tick /
// sched_yield. No externs are declared in this file (the scheduler is
// pure-PM — K4c's context-switch will declare its own externs for CR3
// write, RSP load, IRET, etc.; this file just picks the next task to
// run and updates the runqueue linked list).
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────
// All non-zero constants in this file use DECIMAL per the K4b contract's
// "Use decimal constants" rule. Decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal
// path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties — see
// womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale, and
// K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for the same rule applied to vector=14
// and rip=4096). The constants:
//     256  = "empty" sentinel for runqueue head/tail (also "no next"
//            in Task.next, also "table full" from task_alloc — same
//            sentinel value throughout the scheduler)
//     8    = bytes per u64 field (loop bound for vruntime/next
//            pack/unpack)
//     255  = 0xFF (byte mask for the unpack-shift-and-truncate path)
//     2    = READY state value (set by sched_enqueue on the new task)
//     1    = RUNNING state value (set by sched_dequeue on the picked
//            task)
//     0    = FREE state value (not used by this file, but documented
//            for completeness — K4e's exit/wait writes this on reap)
//   Self-test constants: 100, 50, 200, 3, 0 — all decimal.
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. It re-declares ProcessTable + the 6
// pt_get_* / pt_set_* helpers byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma (same
// pattern as K3d's irq.vuma re-declaring irq_mask/irq_unmask/pic_eoi
// from K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma, and K3e's dispatch.vuma re-declaring
// syscall_table_get / syscall_table_set from table.vuma). K4c (context
// switch) will re-declare Task + TrapFrame + the rest of the pt_get_* /
// pt_set_* helpers; K4d (fork/exec) will re-declare pt_set_ppid /
// pt_set_mm_root / pt_set_fs_root / pt_set_fds.
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/proc/scheduler.vuma \
//         /tmp/scheduler.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/scheduler.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (all 5 self-test checks pass — enqueue 3
// tasks with different vruntimes, dequeue returns lowest-vruntime first
// in CFS order: task 1 (vruntime=50), then task 0 (vruntime=100), then
// task 2 (vruntime=200), then queue is empty).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layouts.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Runqueue: head/tail are Task indices (0..255) or 256 (empty). count is
// the number of tasks currently in the runqueue (0..256). The next-link
// field lives in ProcessTable.nexts (not here) — see file header "Runqueue
// layout" for the rationale.
layout Runqueue = {
    head: u32,   // index of first task in runqueue, or 256 = empty
    tail: u32,   // index of last task, or 256 = empty
    count: u32,  // number of tasks in runqueue
}

// ProcessTable: re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma. 10
// fields (9 parallel flat [u8; N] arrays + count u32) totaling 14340
// bytes. See K4a's task.vuma header "Why parallel flat byte arrays?"
// for the rationale (VUMA has no struct-array support).
layout ProcessTable = {
    pids: [u8; 1024],
    ppids: [u8; 1024],
    states: [u8; 256],
    prios: [u8; 256],
    vruntimes: [u8; 2048],
    mm_roots: [u8; 2048],
    fs_roots: [u8; 2048],
    fds: [u8; 2048],
    nexts: [u8; 2048],
    count: u32,
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ProcessTable helpers (re-declared from K4a's task.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 6 of K4a's 18 helpers, byte-identical to task.vuma. The scheduler only
// needs state (u8 direct-index), vruntime (u64 8-byte LE pack/unpack),
// and next (u64 8-byte LE pack/unpack). The other 12 helpers (pid/ppid
// get/set, prio get/set, mm_root/fs_root/fds get/set) are NOT needed
// here — K4d/K4e will re-declare them when they need them.

// Read the u8 `state` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.states.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
fn pt_get_state(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u8 {
    return tbl.states[idx];
}

// Write the u8 `state` field of Task slot `idx` into ProcessTable.states.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
fn pt_set_state(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u8) {
    tbl.states[idx] = val;
}

// Read the u64 `vruntime` field of Task slot `idx` from
// ProcessTable.vruntimes. Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma
// (8-byte little-endian unpack loop — same pattern as K2a's
// pool_get_base and K3e's syscall_table_get).
fn pt_get_vruntime(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.vruntimes[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `vruntime` field of Task slot `idx` into
// ProcessTable.vruntimes. Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
fn pt_set_vruntime(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.vruntimes[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Read the u64 `next` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.nexts.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma. The scheduler uses
// Task.next to thread the runqueue linked list (head/tail live in the
// Runqueue struct; per-node next-link lives in ProcessTable.nexts).
fn pt_get_next(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.nexts[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `next` field of Task slot `idx` into ProcessTable.nexts.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
fn pt_set_next(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.nexts[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Runqueue operations.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// sched_init: initialize an empty runqueue. Idempotent. Called by kmain
// at boot before the first sched_enqueue. head = tail = 256 (empty
// sentinel), count = 0.
fn sched_init(rq: State<Runqueue>) {
    rq.head = 256;  // empty
    rq.tail = 256;
    rq.count = 0;
}

// sched_enqueue: add task `task_idx` to the tail of the runqueue. Sets
// the task's state to READY (2). Tail-insertion gives FIFO ordering for
// tasks with equal vruntimes (same as Linux's rbtree leftmost-with-tie-
// break-on-insertion-order behavior). Updates Task.next of the old tail
// to point at the new task, and sets the new task's Task.next to 256
// (end-of-list). Bumps rq.count.
//
// The empty-queue case (rq.head == 256) is handled separately: both
// head and tail are set to task_idx, and the new task's next is set to
// 256. The non-empty case appends to the current tail.
fn sched_enqueue(rq: State<Runqueue>, tbl: State<ProcessTable>, task_idx: u32) {
    if rq.head == 256 {
        // Empty queue.
        rq.head = task_idx;
        rq.tail = task_idx;
        pt_set_next(tbl, task_idx, 256);  // no next
    } else {
        // Append to tail.
        pt_set_next(tbl, rq.tail, task_idx);
        rq.tail = task_idx;
        pt_set_next(tbl, task_idx, 256);
    }
    rq.count = rq.count + 1;
    pt_set_state(tbl, task_idx, 2);  // READY
}

// sched_dequeue: remove and return the task with the LOWEST vruntime
// (CFS "pick next" step). Walks the runqueue linked list once to find
// the min-vruntime task (tracking `prev_of_min` so the removal step can
// relink the predecessor), removes it from the list, sets its state to
// RUNNING (1), decrements rq.count, and returns its index.
//
// Returns 256 if the runqueue is empty (the caller MUST check — kmain's
// idle loop uses this to know when to spin on the idle task).
//
// The walk tracks:
//   min_idx       — index of the min-vruntime task found so far
//   min_vruntime  — its vruntime (for comparison)
//   cur           — current list cursor (u64 because pt_get_next
//                   returns u64; cast to u32 for pt_get_* / pt_set_*
//                   calls)
//   prev          — the node immediately before `cur` (u32)
//   prev_of_min   — the node immediately before `min_idx` (u32; stays
//                   256 if `min_idx` is the head — meaning no relink
//                   of a predecessor is needed for removal)
//
// After the walk, the removal step has two cases:
//   (a) min_idx == rq.head: just advance rq.head to min_idx's next.
//       If that makes the queue empty (rq.head == 256), also clear
//       rq.tail to 256.
//   (b) min_idx != rq.head: relink prev_of_min.next to min_idx.next.
//       If min_idx was the tail, also update rq.tail to prev_of_min.
//
// This is byte-identical in structure to K2a's pmm_free buddy-search
// loop (pmm.vuma lines ~475-490) — same `let prev = 256; while cur !=
// 256 { ...; prev = cur; cur = next; }` walk, plus the prev_of_min
// tracking for the removal relink.
fn sched_dequeue(rq: State<Runqueue>, tbl: State<ProcessTable>) -> u32 {
    if rq.head == 256 {
        return 256;  // empty
    }
    // Find the task with min vruntime.
    let min_idx = rq.head;
    let min_vruntime = pt_get_vruntime(tbl, min_idx);
    let cur = pt_get_next(tbl, min_idx);
    let prev_of_min = 256;
    let prev = min_idx;
    while cur != 256 {
        let v = pt_get_vruntime(tbl, cur as u32);
        if v < min_vruntime {
            min_idx = cur as u32;
            prev_of_min = prev;
        }
        prev = cur as u32;
        cur = pt_get_next(tbl, cur as u32);
    }
    // Remove min_idx from the runqueue.
    if min_idx == rq.head {
        rq.head = pt_get_next(tbl, min_idx) as u32;
        if rq.head == 256 {
            rq.tail = 256;
        }
    } else {
        let min_next = pt_get_next(tbl, min_idx);
        pt_set_next(tbl, prev_of_min, min_next);
        if min_idx == rq.tail {
            rq.tail = prev_of_min;
        }
    }
    rq.count = rq.count - 1;
    pt_set_state(tbl, min_idx, 1);  // RUNNING
    return min_idx;
}

// schedule: pick the next task to run. Wraps sched_dequeue (CFS "pick
// min vruntime"). kmain's idle loop calls this after every IRQ; the
// timer-IRQ handler (K4c) calls this after sched_tick on the current
// task. Returns 256 if no tasks are runnable (kmain spins on the idle
// task in that case).
fn schedule(rq: State<Runqueue>, tbl: State<ProcessTable>) -> u32 {
    return sched_dequeue(rq, tbl);
}

// sched_tick: bump task `task_idx`'s vruntime by `delta`. Called by the
// timer-IRQ handler (K4c) on every tick — delta is typically the tick
// interval in nanoseconds (e.g. 1000000 for a 1ms tick at 1000 HZ, or
// 10000000 for a 10ms tick at 100 HZ). The current task's vruntime
// monotonically increases, so it eventually becomes the largest in the
// runqueue, at which point sched_dequeue picks a different task.
//
// This is the CFS fairness mechanism: every task gets a turn, and the
// task that has run the most (highest vruntime) gets passed over in
// favor of the task that has run the least (lowest vruntime).
fn sched_tick(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, task_idx: u32, delta: u64) {
    let v = pt_get_vruntime(tbl, task_idx);
    pt_set_vruntime(tbl, task_idx, v + delta);
}

// sched_yield: re-enqueue the current task (add it to the tail of the
// runqueue) and pick the next task to run (sched_dequeue). Returns the
// next task's index (or 256 if the runqueue is now empty — but that
// can't happen if `current` was just enqueued, so the return is always
// a valid task index). Called by sys_sched_yield (K4f's syscall
// handler) and by K4c's cooperative-yield trampoline.
//
// Note: sched_enqueue sets current's state to READY (2), and the
// following sched_dequeue sets the picked task's state to RUNNING (1).
// If current is picked again (because it still has the lowest vruntime
// — e.g. all other tasks have higher vruntimes), its state goes
// READY → RUNNING. The caller doesn't need to handle this case
// specially: the return value tells it which task to switch to.
fn sched_yield(rq: State<Runqueue>, tbl: State<ProcessTable>, current: u32) -> u32 {
    sched_enqueue(rq, tbl, current);
    return sched_dequeue(rq, tbl);
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test (the K4b DoD).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Allocate a Runqueue and a ProcessTable via state_new, init the
// runqueue via sched_init, then manually set up 3 tasks with different
// vruntimes (Task 0: vruntime=100, Task 1: vruntime=50, Task 2:
// vruntime=200), enqueue all 3, and verify that 3 successive
// sched_dequeue calls return them in CFS order (lowest vruntime first):
//   (1) After enqueuing 0, 1, 2: rq.count == 3.
//   (2) sched_dequeue → returns 1 (vruntime=50, lowest).
//   (3) sched_dequeue → returns 0 (vruntime=100, next lowest).
//   (4) sched_dequeue → returns 2 (vruntime=200, highest).
//   (5) rq.count == 0 (queue drained).
// Returns 0 on success, 1..5 on the corresponding check failure (so a
// future CI failure pinpoints the broken check).
//
// The self-test does NOT call pt_init on the ProcessTable — the states
// array starts zeroed (state_new zeros the ___pmt_buffer backing the
// State<ProcessTable>), so all 256 slots start FREE. The self-test
// manually sets states[0..3] to READY (2) via pt_set_state to simulate
// task_alloc having marked them runnable. K4a's task.vuma self-test
// does the same (uses pt_init + task_alloc; K4b skips the alloc step
// because we're testing the scheduler, not the allocator — the
// scheduler only cares that the tasks it's asked to enqueue are
// runnable, not how they got that way).
//
// Per the K4b contract's "decimal constants" rule: all non-zero
// constants in the self-test use DECIMAL (100, 50, 200, 3, 0) — decimal
// avoids the parser's hex-literal path (parse_int_radix width-extension
// subtleties — see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same
// rationale, and K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for the same rule applied
// to vector=14 and rip=4096).

fn main() -> i32 {
    let rq = state_new(Runqueue);
    let tbl = state_new(ProcessTable);
    sched_init(rq);
    // Manually set up 3 tasks with different vruntimes.
    // Task 0: vruntime=100, Task 1: vruntime=50, Task 2: vruntime=200.
    pt_set_state(tbl, 0, 2);
    pt_set_vruntime(tbl, 0, 100);
    pt_set_state(tbl, 1, 2);
    pt_set_vruntime(tbl, 1, 50);
    pt_set_state(tbl, 2, 2);
    pt_set_vruntime(tbl, 2, 200);
    // Enqueue all 3.
    sched_enqueue(rq, tbl, 0);
    sched_enqueue(rq, tbl, 1);
    sched_enqueue(rq, tbl, 2);
    if rq.count != 3 { return 1; }
    // Dequeue should return task 1 (lowest vruntime = 50).
    let next = sched_dequeue(rq, tbl);
    if next != 1 { return 2; }
    // Dequeue should return task 0 (vruntime = 100).
    let next2 = sched_dequeue(rq, tbl);
    if next2 != 0 { return 3; }
    // Dequeue should return task 2 (vruntime = 200).
    let next3 = sched_dequeue(rq, tbl);
    if next3 != 2 { return 4; }
    // Queue should be empty.
    if rq.count != 0 { return 5; }
    return 0;
}
